{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794851632315,"sessionID":"ses_e1a82d918ffeSg4o2dc6d2Zt3H","part":{"partID":"prt_1e57e7300001u5015BiWCLiLzO","sessionID":"ses_e1a82d918ffeSg4o2dc6d2Zt3H","messageID":"msg_1e57e526b001hDn0f3XJ0eW3z0","type":"tool","id":"call_b3df8bf28d354a17b4ffee84","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts","offset":330,"limit":180},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts, lines 330-509\n330: \n331: // A request that asks for a document to be created or written wants the whole\n332: // document, even when it names a correction to make afterwards, so this check\n333: // stands the small-edit gate down.\n334: const CREATION_REQUEST = /\\b(?:creat(?:e|es|ing)|writ(?:e|es|ing)|author(?:ing)?|draft(?:ing)?|scaffold(?:ing)?|generat(?:e|es|ing)|mak(?:e|es|ing)|new|build(?:ing)?|set up|add an?|need an?)\\b/;\n335: \n336: // A small edit names the correction to make, so the verb is what marks one.\n337: const CORRECTION_VERB = /\\b(?:fix|correct|update|edit|change|tweak|repair)\\b/;\n338: \n339: // These three creation commands write a whole document that is later corrected in\n340: // place, so a correction request names an edit to it rather than a new one. Every\n341: // other creation command names a purpose that a correction word belongs to.\n342: const SMALL_EDIT_GATED_COMMANDS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set(['/create:readme', '/create:feature-catalog', '/create:manual-testing-playbook']);\n343: \n344: // Wording that asks for an answer and rules out a change. One pattern serves\n345: // every gate that has to honour it, so a new gate cannot drift from the first.\n346: // Words alone cannot tell \"make no changes to the files\" from a change request\n347: // that says what to leave alone, so both read as no change: losing an offer\n348: // costs less than offering a mutating command on a prompt that ruled one out.\n349: // A negated write verb counts only with an object that names nothing in\n350: // particular, because \"don't add tools\" limits a change and does not refuse it.\n351: export const NO_EDIT_REQUEST = /\\b(no edits?|make no changes|no changes yet|without making changes|(?:do not|don't|dont) (?:make (?:any )?changes|change|edit|modify|touch|(?:create|write|add|delete|remove) (?:it|this|that|anything|any files?))|read-only only|no edits yet|only; do not|just show|just list)\\b/;\n352: \n353: // Many keyboards type the contraction with a curly apostrophe, so the text is\n354: // folded to the straight form before the pattern reads it.\n355: export function isNoEditRequest(text: string): boolean {\n356:   return NO_EDIT_REQUEST.test(text.toLowerCase().replace(/[\\u2018\\u2019]/g, \"'\"));\n357: }\n358: \n359: // A refusal or a question reaches its command through a use verb, so the two\n360: // lists below share this one. A gap holds one or two plain words and no\n361: // punctuation, because a comma ends the clause that holds the lead.\n362: export const COMMAND_USE_VERB = '(?:run|start|use|invoke|launch|execute|call|trigger|open|rerun|re-run|kick off)';\n363: export const COMMAND_GAP_WORDS = \"(?:[a-z']+ ){0,2}\";\n364: \n365: // `why not` proposes the command rather than refusing it, so the word refuses\n366: // only where no `why` stands before it.\n367: const COMMAND_NOT_WORD = '(?<!\\\\bwhy )not';\n368: \n369: // A command the reader refuses is named without being asked for, so this list\n370: // holds the leads that mark that naming. A lead must sit directly before the\n371: // command so a later clause cannot refuse an earlier request, and a gap holds\n372: // plain words only so a refusal before a comma cannot swallow the request after\n373: // it. A reader who stops using a command names it only to refuse it.\n374: export const COMMAND_DECLINE_LEADS: readonly string[] = [\n375:   `(?:do not|don't|dont|never|${COMMAND_NOT_WORD}|won't|wont|no longer|cannot|can't|no need to|need not|needn't) (?!(?:forget|hesitate|fail|mind)\\\\b)${COMMAND_GAP_WORDS}${COMMAND_USE_VERB} ${COMMAND_GAP_WORDS}`,\n376:   `(?:skip|skipping|without|instead of|rather than|avoid|stop|stopped|quit|${COMMAND_NOT_WORD}) (?:(?!${COMMAND_USE_VERB}\\\\b)[a-z']+ ){0,2}`,\n377: ];\n378: \n379: // A refusal or a question over an or-list covers every command the list joins,\n380: // while a second use verb opens a clause of its own, so the list joins only `or`\n381: // and `, or`. A listed command may carry `the` before it and\n382: // `command` after it, and the command after the last `or` may carry `the`. A\n383: // listed command may also carry one plain word after it, such as the subcommand a\n384: // doctor command takes, so `do not run /x now or /y` refuses both.\n385: const COMMAND_OR_LIST = '(?:(?:the )?\\\\/[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*(?::[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*)?(?: [a-z][a-z0-9_-]*)?(?: command)?,? or (?:the )?)*';\n386: \n387: // A command the reader asks about is named without being asked for, so this list\n388: // holds the leads that mark that naming. A lead must sit directly before the\n389: // command so a later clause cannot turn an earlier request into a question, and\n390: // a gap holds plain words only so a question before a comma cannot swallow the\n391: // request after it.\n392: export const COMMAND_ASK_LEADS: readonly string[] = [\n393:   \"(?:what does|what is|what's|what would|what will|what did|how does|how did|why does|why did|explain|describe|tell me what|tell me how|tell me about|tell me why|difference between|differ from|differs from|different from|undo what|ran|already ran|was running) (?:the )?(?:command )?\",\n394:   `(?:should i|should we|do i need to|when would i) ${COMMAND_USE_VERB} (?:the )?(?:command )?`,\n395: ];\n396: \n397: // A bare ask word opens a statement as often as a question, so it marks a naming\n398: // only when the command it precedes is followed by a question mark before the\n399: // sentence ends, or when the lead itself opens a sentence, which a question mark ends as a full stop does.\n400: // A question often names the command after `the`, as in `is the /x command safe`.\n401: export const COMMAND_BARE_ASK_LEAD = '(?:is|does|can|will|would) (?:the )?';\n402: \n403: // A clause that opens with a use verb and `it` asks for the command an earlier\n404: // clause only named, so a naming such as `not run /x yet, please start it`\n405: // becomes a request. The clause has to open that way so `I will run it` stays a\n406: // statement.\n407: const COMMAND_ASKED_BY_PRONOUN = new RegExp(`(?:^|[,.;!?\\\\n]) *(?:(?:and|then|so|please|now|just|go ahead and) )*${COMMAND_USE_VERB} it\\\\b`);\n408: \n409: // A prompt names a command to refuse it or to ask about it as often as it names\n410: // one to run it, so an offer must read the naming before it trusts the name. The\n411: // text is folded first, because a keyed apostrophe or a run of spaces must not\n412: // hide the lead, while a line break survives as the sentence end it is. Inline\n413: // code, bold and quote marks around a name are dropped for the same reason, so a\n414: // refusal of a backticked command reads as the plain refusal does. An underscore\n415: // stays, because a command name may hold one. A single quote goes only where it\n416: // opens a command name, because the same mark is the apostrophe of a lead such\n417: // as \"don't\" or \"what's\". The\n418: // boundary before the lead keeps a command name from matching inside a word or a\n419: // path, and the boundary after it keeps a longer command name from reading as the\n420: // shorter one. Every occurrence has to carry a lead, because one led naming does\n421: // not turn a later unled naming into a request.\n422: export function commandOnlyMentioned(prompt: string, command: string): boolean {\n423:   const text = prompt\n424:     .toLowerCase()\n425:     .replace(/[`*\"\\u201c\\u201d]/g, '')\n426:     .replace(/[\\u2018\\u2019]/g, \"'\")\n427:     .replace(/(^|[^a-z0-9])'(?=\\/[a-z])/g, '$1')\n428:     .replace(/[^\\S\\n]+/g, ' ')\n429:     .replace(/ *\\n\\s*/g, '\\n');\n430:   const name = escapePhrase(command.toLowerCase());\n431:   const occurrences = [...text.matchAll(new RegExp(`(?:^|[^a-z0-9_/])${name}(?![a-z0-9_-])`, 'g'))];\n432:   if (occurrences.length === 0) return false;\n433:   return occurrences.every((occurrence) => {\n434:     const nameEnd = (occurrence.index ?? 0) + occurrence[0].length;\n435:     const before = text.slice(0, nameEnd);\n436:     const tail = text.slice(nameEnd);\n437:     if (COMMAND_ASKED_BY_PRONOUN.test(tail.split(/(?:^|[\\s,;(])\\/[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*(?::[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*)?(?![a-z0-9_\\/-]|\\.[a-z0-9])/)[0])) return false;\n438:     const led = COMMAND_DECLINE_LEADS.some((lead) => (\n439:       new RegExp(`(?:^|[^a-z0-9_/])${lead}${COMMAND_OR_LIST}${name}$`).test(before)\n440:     )) || COMMAND_ASK_LEADS.some((lead) => (\n441:       new RegExp(`(?:^|[^a-z0-9_/])${lead}${COMMAND_OR_LIST}${name}$`).test(before)\n442:     ));\n443:     if (led) return true;\n444:     const bareLead = new RegExp(`(?:^|[^a-z0-9_/])${COMMAND_BARE_ASK_LEAD}${COMMAND_OR_LIST}${name}$`).test(before);\n445:     if (!bareLead) return false;\n446:     const sentenceEnd = tail.search(/[.!\\n]/);\n447:     const sentence = sentenceEnd === -1 ? tail : tail.slice(0, sentenceEnd);\n448:     // A chat question often leaves the question mark off, so a bare ask word that\n449:     // opens a sentence still asks about the command.\n450:     return sentence.includes('?')\n451:       || new RegExp(`(?:^|[.!?\\\\n] *)${COMMAND_BARE_ASK_LEAD}${COMMAND_OR_LIST}${name}$`).test(before);\n452:   });\n453: }\n454: \n455: // A compiled route arrives as unvalidated JSON from a separate process, so the\n456: // shape is narrowed here rather than trusted. Arrays count as objects in\n457: // JavaScript and would let `action` read off an unrelated field, so they are\n458: // excluded explicitly.\n459: function asRecord(value: unknown): Record<string, unknown> | undefined {\n460:   if (typeof value !== 'object' || value === null || Array.isArray(value)) return undefined;\n461:   return value as Record<string, unknown>;\n462: }\n463: \n464: // The command for a hub's recommendation, or undefined when the route does not\n465: // single out one mode. Only a single-mode route names a command unambiguously:\n466: // a bundle or a multi-target route has more than one answer, and asking the\n467: // reader to pick belongs to the recommend path, not here. Never throws, because\n468: // a missing command must degrade to \"no command offered\" rather than break\n469: // routing. The prompt decides whether a costly deep-loop command is offered at\n470: // all: a reader who only asks about the topic gets no command, rather than one\n471: // that would launch work they never requested.\n472: export function attachCommand(\n473:   skillId: string,\n474:   compiledRoute: unknown,\n475:   prompt: string,\n476:   modeCommands: Readonly<Record<string, Readonly<Record<string, string>>>> = MODE_COMMANDS,\n477: ): string | undefined {\n478:   const route = asRecord(compiledRoute);\n479:   if (!route) return undefined;\n480:   if (route.action !== 'route' || route.selectionKind !== 'single') return undefined;\n481: \n482:   const targets = route.targets;\n483:   if (!Array.isArray(targets) || targets.length !== 1) return undefined;\n484: \n485:   const onlyTarget = asRecord(targets[0]);\n486:   if (!onlyTarget) return undefined;\n487: \n488:   const workflowMode = onlyTarget.workflowMode;\n489:   if (typeof workflowMode !== 'string' || workflowMode === '') return undefined;\n490: \n491:   // A reader who rules out a change gets no command from either path, so the hub\n492:   // route never offers what the scorer already withholds.\n493:   if (isNoEditRequest(prompt)) return undefined;\n494: \n495:   // Own-key lookup on both levels: the table is data read from a generated\n496:   // object, so an inherited name such as `constructor` must not resolve to a\n497:   // command the hub never declared.\n498:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(modeCommands, skillId)) return undefined;\n499:   const commands = modeCommands[skillId];\n500:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(commands, workflowMode)) return undefined;\n501: \n502:   // A route can pick the mode because the prompt named its command, so a naming\n503:   // that refused or asked about the command must not produce the offer.\n504:   if (commandOnlyMentioned(prompt, commands[workflowMode])) return undefined;\n505: \n506:   // A correction inside one document is not a request for a creation workflow,\n507:   // which scaffolds, reviews and validates a whole one.\n508:   if (\n509:     SMALL_EDIT_GATED_COMMANDS.has(commands[workflowMode])\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 510]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts, lines 330-509\n330: \n331: // A request that asks for a document to be created or written wants the whole\n332: // document, even when it names a correction to make afterwards, so this check\n333: // stands the small-edit gate down.\n334: const CREATION_REQUEST = /\\b(?:creat(?:e|es|ing)|writ(?:e|es|ing)|author(?:ing)?|draft(?:ing)?|scaffold(?:ing)?|generat(?:e|es|ing)|mak(?:e|es|ing)|new|build(?:ing)?|set up|add an?|need an?)\\b/;\n335: \n336: // A small edit names the correction to make, so the verb is what marks one.\n337: const CORRECTION_VERB = /\\b(?:fix|correct|update|edit|change|tweak|repair)\\b/;\n338: \n339: // These three creation commands write a whole document that is later corrected in\n340: // place, so a correction request names an edit to it rather than a new one. Every\n341: // other creation command names a purpose that a correction word belongs to.\n342: const SMALL_EDIT_GATED_COMMANDS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set(['/create:readme', '/create:feature-catalog', '/create:manual-testing-playbook']);\n343: \n344: // Wording that asks for an answer and rules out a change. One pattern serves\n345: // every gate that has to honour it, so a new gate cannot drift from the first.\n346: // Words alone cannot tell \"make no changes to the files\" from a change request\n347: // that says what to leave alone, so both read as no change: losing an offer\n348: // costs less than offering a mutating command on a prompt that ruled one out.\n349: // A negated write verb counts only with an object that names nothing in\n350: // particular, because \"don't add tools\" limits a change and does not refuse it.\n351: export const NO_EDIT_REQUEST = /\\b(no edits?|make no changes|no changes yet|without making changes|(?:do not|don't|dont) (?:make (?:any )?changes|change|edit|modify|touch|(?:create|write|add|delete|remove) (?:it|this|that|anything|any files?))|read-only only|no edits yet|only; do not|just show|just list)\\b/;\n352: \n353: // Many keyboards type the contraction with a curly apostrophe, so the text is\n354: // folded to the straight form before the pattern reads it.\n355: export function isNoEditRequest(text: string): boolean {\n356:   return NO_EDIT_REQUEST.test(text.toLowerCase().replace(/[\\u2018\\u2019]/g, \"'\"));\n357: }\n358: \n359: // A refusal or a question reaches its command through a use verb, so the two\n360: // lists below share this one. A gap holds one or two plain words and no\n361: // punctuation, because a comma ends the clause that holds the lead.\n362: export const COMMAND_USE_VERB = '(?:run|start|use|invoke|launch|execute|call|trigger|open|rerun|re-run|kick off)';\n363: export const COMMAND_GAP_WORDS = \"(?:[a-z']+ ){0,2}\";\n364: \n365: // `why not` proposes the command rather than refusing it, so the word refuses\n366: // only where no `why` stands before it.\n367: const COMMAND_NOT_WORD = '(?<!\\\\bwhy )not';\n368: \n369: // A command the reader refuses is named without being asked for, so this list\n370: // holds the leads that mark that naming. A lead must sit directly before the\n371: // command so a later clause cannot refuse an earlier request, and a gap holds\n372: // plain words only so a refusal before a comma cannot swallow the request after\n373: // it. A reader who stops using a command names it only to refuse it.\n374: export const COMMAND_DECLINE_LEADS: readonly string[] = [\n375:   `(?:do not|don't|dont|never|${COMMAND_NOT_WORD}|won't|wont|no longer|cannot|can't|no need to|need not|needn't) (?!(?:forget|hesitate|fail|mind)\\\\b)${COMMAND_GAP_WORDS}${COMMAND_USE_VERB} ${COMMAND_GAP_WORDS}`,\n376:   `(?:skip|skipping|without|instead of|rather than|avoid|stop|stopped|quit|${COMMAND_NOT_WORD}) (?:(?!${COMMAND_USE_VERB}\\\\b)[a-z']+ ){0,2}`,\n377: ];\n378: \n379: // A refusal or a question over an or-list covers every command the list joins,\n380: // while a second use verb opens a clause of its own, so the list joins only `or`\n381: // and `, or`. A listed command may carry `the` before it and\n382: // `command` after it, and the command after the last `or` may carry `the`. A\n383: // listed command may also carry one plain word after it, such as the subcommand a\n384: // doctor command takes, so `do not run /x now or /y` refuses both.\n385: const COMMAND_OR_LIST = '(?:(?:the )?\\\\/[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*(?::[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*)?(?: [a-z][a-z0-9_-]*)?(?: command)?,? or (?:the )?)*';\n386: \n387: // A command the reader asks about is named without being asked for, so this list\n388: // holds the leads that mark that naming. A lead must sit directly before the\n389: // command so a later clause cannot turn an earlier request into a question, and\n390: // a gap holds plain words only so a question before a comma cannot swallow the\n391: // request after it.\n392: export const COMMAND_ASK_LEADS: readonly string[] = [\n393:   \"(?:what does|what is|what's|what would|what will|what did|how does|how did|why does|why did|explain|describe|tell me what|tell me how|tell me about|tell me why|difference between|differ from|differs from|different from|undo what|ran|already ran|was running) (?:the )?(?:command )?\",\n394:   `(?:should i|should we|do i need to|when would i) ${COMMAND_USE_VERB} (?:the )?(?:command )?`,\n395: ];\n396: \n397: // A bare ask word opens a statement as often as a question, so it marks a naming\n398: // only when the command it precedes is followed by a question mark before the\n399: // sentence ends, or when the lead itself opens a sentence, which a question mark ends as a full stop does.\n400: // A question often names the command after `the`, as in `is the /x command safe`.\n401: export const COMMAND_BARE_ASK_LEAD = '(?:is|does|can|will|would) (?:the )?';\n402: \n403: // A clause that opens with a use verb and `it` asks for the command an earlier\n404: // clause only named, so a naming such as `not run /x yet, please start it`\n405: // becomes a request. The clause has to open that way so `I will run it` stays a\n406: // statement.\n407: const COMMAND_ASKED_BY_PRONOUN = new RegExp(`(?:^|[,.;!?\\\\n]) *(?:(?:and|then|so|please|now|just|go ahead and) )*${COMMAND_USE_VERB} it\\\\b`);\n408: \n409: // A prompt names a command to refuse it or to ask about it as often as it names\n410: // one to run it, so an offer must read the naming before it trusts the name. The\n411: // text is folded first, because a keyed apostrophe or a run of spaces must not\n412: // hide the lead, while a line break survives as the sentence end it is. Inline\n413: // code, bold and quote marks around a name are dropped for the same reason, so a\n414: // refusal of a backticked command reads as the plain refusal does. An underscore\n415: // stays, because a command name may hold one. A single quote goes only where it\n416: // opens a command name, because the same mark is the apostrophe of a lead such\n417: // as \"don't\" or \"what's\". The\n418: // boundary before the lead keeps a command name from matching inside a word or a\n419: // path, and the boundary after it keeps a longer command name from reading as the\n420: // shorter one. Every occurrence has to carry a lead, because one led naming does\n421: // not turn a later unled naming into a request.\n422: export function commandOnlyMentioned(prompt: string, command: string): boolean {\n423:   const text = prompt\n424:     .toLowerCase()\n425:     .replace(/[`*\"\\u201c\\u201d]/g, '')\n426:     .replace(/[\\u2018\\u2019]/g, \"'\")\n427:     .replace(/(^|[^a-z0-9])'(?=\\/[a-z])/g, '$1')\n428:     .replace(/[^\\S\\n]+/g, ' ')\n429:     .replace(/ *\\n\\s*/g, '\\n');\n430:   const name = escapePhrase(command.toLowerCase());\n431:   const occurrences = [...text.matchAll(new RegExp(`(?:^|[^a-z0-9_/])${name}(?![a-z0-9_-])`, 'g'))];\n432:   if (occurrences.length === 0) return false;\n433:   return occurrences.every((occurrence) => {\n434:     const nameEnd = (occurrence.index ?? 0) + occurrence[0].length;\n435:     const before = text.slice(0, nameEnd);\n436:     const tail = text.slice(nameEnd);\n437:     if (COMMAND_ASKED_BY_PRONOUN.test(tail.split(/(?:^|[\\s,;(])\\/[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*(?::[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*)?(?![a-z0-9_\\/-]|\\.[a-z0-9])/)[0])) return false;\n438:     const led = COMMAND_DECLINE_LEADS.some((lead) => (\n439:       new RegExp(`(?:^|[^a-z0-9_/])${lead}${COMMAND_OR_LIST}${name}$`).test(before)\n440:     )) || COMMAND_ASK_LEADS.some((lead) => (\n441:       new RegExp(`(?:^|[^a-z0-9_/])${lead}${COMMAND_OR_LIST}${name}$`).test(before)\n442:     ));\n443:     if (led) return true;\n444:     const bareLead = new RegExp(`(?:^|[^a-z0-9_/])${COMMAND_BARE_ASK_LEAD}${COMMAND_OR_LIST}${name}$`).test(before);\n445:     if (!bareLead) return false;\n446:     const sentenceEnd = tail.search(/[.!\\n]/);\n447:     const sentence = sentenceEnd === -1 ? tail : tail.slice(0, sentenceEnd);\n448:     // A chat question often leaves the question mark off, so a bare ask word that\n449:     // opens a sentence still asks about the command.\n450:     return sentence.includes('?')\n451:       || new RegExp(`(?:^|[.!?\\\\n] *)${COMMAND_BARE_ASK_LEAD}${COMMAND_OR_LIST}${name}$`).test(before);\n452:   });\n453: }\n454: \n455: // A compiled route arrives as unvalidated JSON from a separate process, so the\n456: // shape is narrowed here rather than trusted. Arrays count as objects in\n457: // JavaScript and would let `action` read off an unrelated field, so they are\n458: // excluded explicitly.\n459: function asRecord(value: unknown): Record<string, unknown> | undefined {\n460:   if (typeof value !== 'object' || value === null || Array.isArray(value)) return undefined;\n461:   return value as Record<string, unknown>;\n462: }\n463: \n464: // The command for a hub's recommendation, or undefined when the route does not\n465: // single out one mode. Only a single-mode route names a command unambiguously:\n466: // a bundle or a multi-target route has more than one answer, and asking the\n467: // reader to pick belongs to the recommend path, not here. Never throws, because\n468: // a missing command must degrade to \"no command offered\" rather than break\n469: // routing. The prompt decides whether a costly deep-loop command is offered at\n470: // all: a reader who only asks about the topic gets no command, rather than one\n471: // that would launch work they never requested.\n472: export function attachCommand(\n473:   skillId: string,\n474:   compiledRoute: unknown,\n475:   prompt: string,\n476:   modeCommands: Readonly<Record<string, Readonly<Record<string, string>>>> = MODE_COMMANDS,\n477: ): string | undefined {\n478:   const route = asRecord(compiledRoute);\n479:   if (!route) return undefined;\n480:   if (route.action !== 'route' || route.selectionKind !== 'single') return undefined;\n481: \n482:   const targets = route.targets;\n483:   if (!Array.isArray(targets) || targets.length !== 1) return undefined;\n484: \n485:   const onlyTarget = asRecord(targets[0]);\n486:   if (!onlyTarget) return undefined;\n487: \n488:   const workflowMode = onlyTarget.workflowMode;\n489:   if (typeof workflowMode !== 'string' || workflowMode === '') return undefined;\n490: \n491:   // A reader who rules out a change gets no command from either path, so the hub\n492:   // route never offers what the scorer already withholds.\n493:   if (isNoEditRequest(prompt)) return undefined;\n494: \n495:   // Own-key lookup on both levels: the table is data read from a generated\n496:   // object, so an inherited name such as `constructor` must not resolve to a\n497:   // command the hub never declared.\n498:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(modeCommands, skillId)) return undefined;\n499:   const commands = modeCommands[skillId];\n500:   if (!Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(commands, workflowMode)) return undefined;\n501: \n502:   // A route can pick the mode because the prompt named its command, so a naming\n503:   // that refused or asked about the command must not produce the offer.\n504:   if (commandOnlyMentioned(prompt, commands[workflowMode])) return undefined;\n505: \n506:   // A correction inside one document is not a request for a creation workflow,\n507:   // which scaffolds, reviews and validates a whole one.\n508:   if (\n509:     SMALL_EDIT_GATED_COMMANDS.has(commands[workflowMode])\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 510]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts\",\"offset\":330,\"limit\":180}"},"time":{"start":1794851631872,"end":1794851632315}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794851632338,"sessionID":"ses_e1a82d918ffeSg4o2dc6d2Zt3H","part":{"partID":"prt_1e57e6f7b001SbN11h6Gc1tlo7","sessionID":"ses_e1a82d918ffeSg4o2dc6d2Zt3H","messageID":"msg_1e57e526b001hDn0f3XJ0eW3z0","type":"tool","id":"call_afdaf0b2e2e14c8793874e69","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && wc -l .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts .skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install-presentation.txt .skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install.yaml .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/tests/doctor-install-contract.test.cjs"},"output":"     543 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts\n     531 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts\n     204 .skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install-presentation.txt\n      70 .skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install.yaml\n     320 .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/tests/doctor-install-contract.test.cjs\n    1668 total\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"     543 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts\n     531 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts\n     204 .skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install-presentation.txt\n      70 .skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install.yaml\n     320 .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/tests/doctor-install-contract.test.cjs\n    1668 total\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && wc -l .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts .skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install-presentation.txt .skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install.yaml .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/tests/doctor-install-contract.test.cjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794851630971,"end":1794851632338}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794851633464,"sessionID":"ses_e1a82d918ffeSg4o2dc6d2Zt3H","part":{"id":"prt_1e57e7938001XRFdKUv6LH71Dy","sessionID":"ses_e1a82d918ffeSg4o2dc6d2Zt3H","messageID":"msg_1e57e526b001hDn0f3XJ0eW3z0","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"54ddf6a978af86fe5472976852d7ca646d55ac8a","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":2229,"output":233,"reasoning":398,"cache":{"read":58496,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794851637083,"sessionID":"ses_e1a82d918ffeSg4o2dc6d2Zt3H","part":{"id":"prt_1e57e875b001JDlibjmHy3OIE0","sessionID":"ses_e1a82d918ffeSg4o2dc6d2Zt3H","messageID":"msg_1e57e793d001tT0534L2tH32e4","type":"step-start","snapshot":"54ddf6a978af86fe5472976852d7ca646d55ac8a"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794851637608,"sessionID":"ses_e1a82d918ffeSg4o2dc6d2Zt3H","part":{"partID":"prt_1e57e8870001fNDOFTlOX35Z4B","sessionID":"ses_e1a82d918ffeSg4o2dc6d2Zt3H","messageID":"msg_1e57e793d001tT0534L2tH32e4","type":"tool","id":"call_477b271d6f16429c8c5b0cd2","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install-presentation.txt"},"output":"Read file .skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install-presentation.txt, lines 1-204\n1: # /doctor:install Presentation Contract\n2: \n3: This file is the single source of truth for user-facing presentation in `/doctor:install`: the argument failure, the build check, the install prompt, the symlink check, the ordered steps, the caveat line, result templates and next-step text. The command router owns flag binding and asset loading. The workflow YAML owns execution behavior.\n4: \n5: This command only reads. Every step that writes is printed with its exact command, and the operator runs it.\n6: \n7: Write for someone who has just cloned Skilled and wants it in their own project: plain words, short sentences, one thing at a time.\n8: \n9: ## 1. Argument Failure\n10: \n11: An unknown argument fails before any workflow YAML loads and prints the wording below with the bracketed parts filled in, then ends with the status line. Nothing is read and nothing is changed.\n12: \n13: | Situation | Wording |\n14: |-----------|---------|\n15: | A flag other than `--root <dir>`, `--runtimes <list>` and `--json` | `Unknown argument: [argument]` then `/doctor:install takes --root <dir>, --runtimes <list> and --json, or list and ?.` then `STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"unknown_argument\"` |\n16: | `--root` or `--runtimes` with no value after it | The same two lines, then `STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"unknown_argument\"` |\n17: | A second positional token | The same two lines, then `STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"unknown_argument\"` |\n18: \n19: `list`, `?` and `menu` as the whole argument print the steps in section 6, with `<dir>`, `<list>` and `<shape>` left as they stand, then stop. They load no workflow and inspect nothing.\n20: \n21: ## 2. The Build Check\n22: \n23: The Skilled checkout is the repository this command runs in. The runtimes load two builds from it, so check both before anything is planned.\n24: \n25: | Build | Check, run from the checkout root | Built when |\n26: |-------|-----------------------------------|------------|\n27: | Spec Kit | `test -d .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist && test -f .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js` | exit 0 |\n28: | Skill Advisor | `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs list-tools --format json` | exit 0 |\n29: \n30: Render both lines, then carry on either way: a missing build never stops the guide.\n31: \n32: ```text\n33: DOCTOR INSTALL — BUILDS\n34: Source: [the Skilled checkout]\n35: Spec Kit build: [found at .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist and runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js | missing]\n36: Advisor CLI: [answers list-tools | missing or not built]\n37: ```\n38: \n39: A missing build becomes step 0 of the checklist. Step 0 carries the README's build commands word for word:\n40: \n41: ```bash\n42: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit install\n43: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit run build\n44: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime install\n45: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n46: ```\n47: \n48: Step 0 runs from the Skilled checkout. Its why line is: `Why: the session hooks and the advisor read these builds, so nothing else works until they are there.` It has no mutation class because no doctor route owns it.\n49: \n50: ## 3. The Install Prompt\n51: \n52: Ask every answer that is still missing in one prompt, then wait. This follows the consolidated question protocol. Questions 1 and 2 are dropped when `--root` and `--runtimes` were passed. The link shape and the instruction choice have no flag, so they are always in the prompt.\n53: \n54: ```text\n55: Where should Skilled go? Answer any or all in one reply, for example \"/Users/me/code/my-app, claude and codex, auto, embed\".\n56: \n57: 1) Target repo: the absolute path, for example /Users/me/code/my-app\n58: 2) Runtimes: claude, opencode, codex, cursor, devin, pi, vibe, dsh, or \"the ones already there\"\n59: 3) Link shape: auto / whole / merge   (auto unless a real folder already sits where a link would go)\n60: 4) Instruction file: embed / link   (embed keeps the repo's own AGENTS.md; link points AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md and REPO RULES.md at the checkout's copies)\n61: ```\n62: \n63: The example carries all four answers so the shape of a full reply is visible. Never print a question the flags already answered.\n64: \n65: ## 4. Accepted Answers\n66: \n67: | Answer | Binds | Meaning |\n68: |--------|-------|---------|\n69: | An absolute path | `--root <dir>` | The project repo that receives the links. The path must exist and be a directory; the engine exits 2 and names it otherwise |\n70: | `claude`, `claude and codex`, `claude,opencode` | `--runtimes <list>` | The runtime folders to link, as a comma list |\n71: | `the ones already there`, empty, `any` | no `--runtimes` | The engine keeps the runtimes its own detection finds in the root, and always links `.skilled` |\n72: | `auto` | `--mode auto` | Whole link where the path is free, merge where a real folder is in the way. The one to pick |\n73: | `whole` | `--mode whole` | One link per entry, refused where a real folder exists |\n74: | `merge` | `--mode merge` | A real folder whose children are links. A local file already in the folder is kept and reported, never overwritten |\n75: | `embed` | step 2 stays | The repo keeps its own `AGENTS.md` and the shipped sections are refreshed into it |\n76: | `link` | step 1 gains `--link-instructions`, step 2 is dropped | `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `REPO RULES.md` become links into the checkout |\n77: \n78: A runtime name outside claude, codex, cursor, devin, dsh, opencode, pi and vibe or the ones already there, a shape outside the three, or an instruction word outside the two is not guessed. Ask that one question again with the accepted values. When the engine refuses a root, show its `ERROR:` line as it printed and ask question 1 again.\n79: \n80: ## 5. The Symlink Check\n81: \n82: Run `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/symlink-engine.cjs check --root <dir> [--runtimes <list>] [--link-instructions]` once, in place and read-only, then render its entry lines as they print:\n83: \n84: ```text\n85: DOCTOR INSTALL — WHAT IS ALREADY THERE\n86: Root: [the target repo]\n87: [the engine's entry lines, verbatim, with its indented detail lines and its repo-level warnings]\n88: ```\n89: \n90: Do not re-sort the lines, re-word a status or sum them into a verdict. A `missing` line is work for step 1, not a failure, and the check still exits 0 for it. The check exits 1 only on drift: a broken or foreign link, including a broken link inside a merged folder, a missing source or a tracked absolute link. Missing children in a partial merge do not count as drift. Add `--link-instructions` when the instruction choice is `link`.\n91: \n92: | Status | What it means for this install |\n93: |--------|-------------------------------|\n94: | `linked-whole` | Already a link into the checkout. Step 1 has nothing to do for it |\n95: | `linked-merge` | A real folder whose children are links. Nothing to do |\n96: | `partial-merge` | A real folder missing some links. Step 1 fills the gaps and never deletes a local file |\n97: | `missing` | Not there yet. Step 1 creates it |\n98: | `broken` | A link whose target is gone. Step 1 replaces it |\n99: | `foreign-link` | A link that points somewhere else. Left alone, and the engine refuses to touch it |\n100: | `local` | A real file or folder of the repo's own. Left alone |\n101: | `source-missing` | The checkout has no such path. Nothing is planned |\n102: \n103: ## 6. The Ordered Steps\n104: \n105: This is the checklist, and it is also what `list`, `?` and `menu` print. Each step carries its exact command, its class and one line of why.\n106: \n107: The class column is the manifest's `mutating` value for the route that owns the printed command, verbatim, so it is read from the route table and never guessed. A command with no route of its own prints `n/a`.\n108: \n109: ```text\n110: 1) /doctor:symlink link --root <dir> --runtimes <list> --mode <shape>   [class]\n111:    Why: creates the links. It shows a plan first and asks before it writes.\n112: \n113: 2) /doctor:agents-md embed --root <dir>   [class]\n114:    Why: keeps the repo's own AGENTS.md and refreshes the shipped sections into it. Dropped when the instruction choice is link.\n115: \n116: 3) /doctor:mcp install   [class]\n117:    Why: builds and registers Code Mode. Run it from the Skilled checkout.\n118: \n119: 4) /doctor:git setup   [class]\n120:    Why: checks the git hooks and sk-git settings, and names the repair for each one that is off. Run it from the target repo.\n121: \n122: 5) /doctor:skill-advisor embedder   [class]\n123:    Why: sets up meaning matching for commands. It shows the cost and asks before it pulls the model or starts the server. Run it from the target repo.\n124: \n125: 6) /doctor:setup   [class]\n126:    Why: confirms the result. Run it from the target repo.\n127: ```\n128: \n129: Run them in this order. Step 1 takes `--link-instructions` when the instruction choice is `link`.\n130: \n131: When the workflow renders the checklist after the check, print this header above the steps:\n132: \n133: ```text\n134: DOCTOR INSTALL — [the target repo]\n135: Source: [the Skilled checkout]\n136: Runtimes: [the runtime list]   Link shape: [auto|whole|merge]   Instruction file: [embed|link]\n137: ```\n138: \n139: A printed step is handed back, never run: each writing command shows its own plan, asks its own yes and writes under its own gate. This command adds no argument to a printed command beyond the values the operator gave.\n140: \n141: ## 7. The Caveat Line\n142: \n143: Print this one line word for word after the steps, on every run, before the result. It is fixed: do not shorten it and do not rewrite it in the operator's words.\n144: \n145: ```text\n146: context saves land in the target repo's own specs/ when they run from inside it, so run /doctor:setup and later saves from inside the target repo, not from the Skilled checkout.\n147: ```\n148: \n149: ## 8. Result Templates\n150: \n151: ### Summary\n152: \n153: ```text\n154: DOCTOR INSTALL RESULT\n155: Source: [the Skilled checkout]\n156: Root: [the target repo]\n157: Runtimes: [the runtime list]\n158: Link shape: [auto|whole|merge]\n159: Instruction file: [embed|link]\n160: Builds: [both found | missing: <which>, printed as step 0]\n161: Already there: [n] linked, [n] missing, [n] to repair\n162: Steps: [n] printed, [n] of them write\n163: Next: run step 1 from the Skilled checkout, then the rest in order\n164: STATUS=OK STEPS=[n]\n165: ```\n166: \n167: ### JSON\n168: \n169: `--json` prints one JSON object and nothing else: no build lines, no steps, no caveat, no header.\n170: \n171: | Key | Value |\n172: |-----|-------|\n173: | `source` | The Skilled checkout's absolute path |\n174: | `root` | The target repo's absolute path |\n175: | `runtimes` | The runtime names, as strings |\n176: | `builds` | One entry per probe in section 2, true or false |\n177: | `symlink_check` | The engine's `check --json` object, unchanged |\n178: | `steps` | One object per printed step: `order`, `command`, `mutating`, `why` |\n179: \n180: ### Failure Summary\n181: \n182: ```text\n183: DOCTOR TARGET FAILED\n184: Target: guide\n185: Step: [the step that failed]\n186: Error: [message]\n187: Next: [the accepted invocation, or the command to rerun]\n188: STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"[message]\"\n189: ```\n190: \n191: ## 9. Troubleshooting Display\n192: \n193: | Situation | Suggested Action |\n194: |-----------|------------------|\n195: | The engine exits 2 | Bad invocation or a refused root. Its first line names the flag or the path. Fix that one thing and rerun the same command |\n196: | The root is the checkout, sits inside it, or holds it | The engine refuses it, because linking a checkout into itself would loop. Name the project repo that should receive the links |\n197: | A build is missing | Run the four `npm` commands in section 2 from the checkout, then rerun this guide. They also print as step 0 |\n198: | A step prints `n/a` as its class | That printed command has no route in the manifest yet. The command still works; only its class is unknown |\n199: | A line reads `local` or `foreign-link` | Left alone on purpose. Move that file or link yourself, or bring it under step 1's shape, and rerun the guide |\n200: | Only the steps are wanted | `list` prints them with no build check and no inspection |\n201: | A saved session lands in the checkout's `specs/` | The save ran from the Skilled checkout. Run it again from inside the target repo, as the caveat in section 7 says |\n202: | The project copies Skilled in instead of linking it | This guide covers linking only. A copy that keeps its own changes keeps them as overlays: run `/doctor:overlay status --root <copy>`, and see the overlays reference in `system-skilled-doctor` for adopting the engine when the project already has its own overlay folder |\n203: \n204: End interactive presentations with: `What would you like to do next?`","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install-presentation.txt, lines 1-204\n1: # /doctor:install Presentation Contract\n2: \n3: This file is the single source of truth for user-facing presentation in `/doctor:install`: the argument failure, the build check, the install prompt, the symlink check, the ordered steps, the caveat line, result templates and next-step text. The command router owns flag binding and asset loading. The workflow YAML owns execution behavior.\n4: \n5: This command only reads. Every step that writes is printed with its exact command, and the operator runs it.\n6: \n7: Write for someone who has just cloned Skilled and wants it in their own project: plain words, short sentences, one thing at a time.\n8: \n9: ## 1. Argument Failure\n10: \n11: An unknown argument fails before any workflow YAML loads and prints the wording below with the bracketed parts filled in, then ends with the status line. Nothing is read and nothing is changed.\n12: \n13: | Situation | Wording |\n14: |-----------|---------|\n15: | A flag other than `--root <dir>`, `--runtimes <list>` and `--json` | `Unknown argument: [argument]` then `/doctor:install takes --root <dir>, --runtimes <list> and --json, or list and ?.` then `STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"unknown_argument\"` |\n16: | `--root` or `--runtimes` with no value after it | The same two lines, then `STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"unknown_argument\"` |\n17: | A second positional token | The same two lines, then `STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"unknown_argument\"` |\n18: \n19: `list`, `?` and `menu` as the whole argument print the steps in section 6, with `<dir>`, `<list>` and `<shape>` left as they stand, then stop. They load no workflow and inspect nothing.\n20: \n21: ## 2. The Build Check\n22: \n23: The Skilled checkout is the repository this command runs in. The runtimes load two builds from it, so check both before anything is planned.\n24: \n25: | Build | Check, run from the checkout root | Built when |\n26: |-------|-----------------------------------|------------|\n27: | Spec Kit | `test -d .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist && test -f .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js` | exit 0 |\n28: | Skill Advisor | `node .skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs list-tools --format json` | exit 0 |\n29: \n30: Render both lines, then carry on either way: a missing build never stops the guide.\n31: \n32: ```text\n33: DOCTOR INSTALL — BUILDS\n34: Source: [the Skilled checkout]\n35: Spec Kit build: [found at .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist and runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js | missing]\n36: Advisor CLI: [answers list-tools | missing or not built]\n37: ```\n38: \n39: A missing build becomes step 0 of the checklist. Step 0 carries the README's build commands word for word:\n40: \n41: ```bash\n42: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit install\n43: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit run build\n44: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime install\n45: npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build\n46: ```\n47: \n48: Step 0 runs from the Skilled checkout. Its why line is: `Why: the session hooks and the advisor read these builds, so nothing else works until they are there.` It has no mutation class because no doctor route owns it.\n49: \n50: ## 3. The Install Prompt\n51: \n52: Ask every answer that is still missing in one prompt, then wait. This follows the consolidated question protocol. Questions 1 and 2 are dropped when `--root` and `--runtimes` were passed. The link shape and the instruction choice have no flag, so they are always in the prompt.\n53: \n54: ```text\n55: Where should Skilled go? Answer any or all in one reply, for example \"/Users/me/code/my-app, claude and codex, auto, embed\".\n56: \n57: 1) Target repo: the absolute path, for example /Users/me/code/my-app\n58: 2) Runtimes: claude, opencode, codex, cursor, devin, pi, vibe, dsh, or \"the ones already there\"\n59: 3) Link shape: auto / whole / merge   (auto unless a real folder already sits where a link would go)\n60: 4) Instruction file: embed / link   (embed keeps the repo's own AGENTS.md; link points AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md and REPO RULES.md at the checkout's copies)\n61: ```\n62: \n63: The example carries all four answers so the shape of a full reply is visible. Never print a question the flags already answered.\n64: \n65: ## 4. Accepted Answers\n66: \n67: | Answer | Binds | Meaning |\n68: |--------|-------|---------|\n69: | An absolute path | `--root <dir>` | The project repo that receives the links. The path must exist and be a directory; the engine exits 2 and names it otherwise |\n70: | `claude`, `claude and codex`, `claude,opencode` | `--runtimes <list>` | The runtime folders to link, as a comma list |\n71: | `the ones already there`, empty, `any` | no `--runtimes` | The engine keeps the runtimes its own detection finds in the root, and always links `.skilled` |\n72: | `auto` | `--mode auto` | Whole link where the path is free, merge where a real folder is in the way. The one to pick |\n73: | `whole` | `--mode whole` | One link per entry, refused where a real folder exists |\n74: | `merge` | `--mode merge` | A real folder whose children are links. A local file already in the folder is kept and reported, never overwritten |\n75: | `embed` | step 2 stays | The repo keeps its own `AGENTS.md` and the shipped sections are refreshed into it |\n76: | `link` | step 1 gains `--link-instructions`, step 2 is dropped | `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md` and `REPO RULES.md` become links into the checkout |\n77: \n78: A runtime name outside claude, codex, cursor, devin, dsh, opencode, pi and vibe or the ones already there, a shape outside the three, or an instruction word outside the two is not guessed. Ask that one question again with the accepted values. When the engine refuses a root, show its `ERROR:` line as it printed and ask question 1 again.\n79: \n80: ## 5. The Symlink Check\n81: \n82: Run `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/symlink-engine.cjs check --root <dir> [--runtimes <list>] [--link-instructions]` once, in place and read-only, then render its entry lines as they print:\n83: \n84: ```text\n85: DOCTOR INSTALL — WHAT IS ALREADY THERE\n86: Root: [the target repo]\n87: [the engine's entry lines, verbatim, with its indented detail lines and its repo-level warnings]\n88: ```\n89: \n90: Do not re-sort the lines, re-word a status or sum them into a verdict. A `missing` line is work for step 1, not a failure, and the check still exits 0 for it. The check exits 1 only on drift: a broken or foreign link, including a broken link inside a merged folder, a missing source or a tracked absolute link. Missing children in a partial merge do not count as drift. Add `--link-instructions` when the instruction choice is `link`.\n91: \n92: | Status | What it means for this install |\n93: |--------|-------------------------------|\n94: | `linked-whole` | Already a link into the checkout. Step 1 has nothing to do for it |\n95: | `linked-merge` | A real folder whose children are links. Nothing to do |\n96: | `partial-merge` | A real folder missing some links. Step 1 fills the gaps and never deletes a local file |\n97: | `missing` | Not there yet. Step 1 creates it |\n98: | `broken` | A link whose target is gone. Step 1 replaces it |\n99: | `foreign-link` | A link that points somewhere else. Left alone, and the engine refuses to touch it |\n100: | `local` | A real file or folder of the repo's own. Left alone |\n101: | `source-missing` | The checkout has no such path. Nothing is planned |\n102: \n103: ## 6. The Ordered Steps\n104: \n105: This is the checklist, and it is also what `list`, `?` and `menu` print. Each step carries its exact command, its class and one line of why.\n106: \n107: The class column is the manifest's `mutating` value for the route that owns the printed command, verbatim, so it is read from the route table and never guessed. A command with no route of its own prints `n/a`.\n108: \n109: ```text\n110: 1) /doctor:symlink link --root <dir> --runtimes <list> --mode <shape>   [class]\n111:    Why: creates the links. It shows a plan first and asks before it writes.\n112: \n113: 2) /doctor:agents-md embed --root <dir>   [class]\n114:    Why: keeps the repo's own AGENTS.md and refreshes the shipped sections into it. Dropped when the instruction choice is link.\n115: \n116: 3) /doctor:mcp install   [class]\n117:    Why: builds and registers Code Mode. Run it from the Skilled checkout.\n118: \n119: 4) /doctor:git setup   [class]\n120:    Why: checks the git hooks and sk-git settings, and names the repair for each one that is off. Run it from the target repo.\n121: \n122: 5) /doctor:skill-advisor embedder   [class]\n123:    Why: sets up meaning matching for commands. It shows the cost and asks before it pulls the model or starts the server. Run it from the target repo.\n124: \n125: 6) /doctor:setup   [class]\n126:    Why: confirms the result. Run it from the target repo.\n127: ```\n128: \n129: Run them in this order. Step 1 takes `--link-instructions` when the instruction choice is `link`.\n130: \n131: When the workflow renders the checklist after the check, print this header above the steps:\n132: \n133: ```text\n134: DOCTOR INSTALL — [the target repo]\n135: Source: [the Skilled checkout]\n136: Runtimes: [the runtime list]   Link shape: [auto|whole|merge]   Instruction file: [embed|link]\n137: ```\n138: \n139: A printed step is handed back, never run: each writing command shows its own plan, asks its own yes and writes under its own gate. This command adds no argument to a printed command beyond the values the operator gave.\n140: \n141: ## 7. The Caveat Line\n142: \n143: Print this one line word for word after the steps, on every run, before the result. It is fixed: do not shorten it and do not rewrite it in the operator's words.\n144: \n145: ```text\n146: context saves land in the target repo's own specs/ when they run from inside it, so run /doctor:setup and later saves from inside the target repo, not from the Skilled checkout.\n147: ```\n148: \n149: ## 8. Result Templates\n150: \n151: ### Summary\n152: \n153: ```text\n154: DOCTOR INSTALL RESULT\n155: Source: [the Skilled checkout]\n156: Root: [the target repo]\n157: Runtimes: [the runtime list]\n158: Link shape: [auto|whole|merge]\n159: Instruction file: [embed|link]\n160: Builds: [both found | missing: <which>, printed as step 0]\n161: Already there: [n] linked, [n] missing, [n] to repair\n162: Steps: [n] printed, [n] of them write\n163: Next: run step 1 from the Skilled checkout, then the rest in order\n164: STATUS=OK STEPS=[n]\n165: ```\n166: \n167: ### JSON\n168: \n169: `--json` prints one JSON object and nothing else: no build lines, no steps, no caveat, no header.\n170: \n171: | Key | Value |\n172: |-----|-------|\n173: | `source` | The Skilled checkout's absolute path |\n174: | `root` | The target repo's absolute path |\n175: | `runtimes` | The runtime names, as strings |\n176: | `builds` | One entry per probe in section 2, true or false |\n177: | `symlink_check` | The engine's `check --json` object, unchanged |\n178: | `steps` | One object per printed step: `order`, `command`, `mutating`, `why` |\n179: \n180: ### Failure Summary\n181: \n182: ```text\n183: DOCTOR TARGET FAILED\n184: Target: guide\n185: Step: [the step that failed]\n186: Error: [message]\n187: Next: [the accepted invocation, or the command to rerun]\n188: STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"[message]\"\n189: ```\n190: \n191: ## 9. Troubleshooting Display\n192: \n193: | Situation | Suggested Action |\n194: |-----------|------------------|\n195: | The engine exits 2 | Bad invocation or a refused root. Its first line names the flag or the path. Fix that one thing and rerun the same command |\n196: | The root is the checkout, sits inside it, or holds it | The engine refuses it, because linking a checkout into itself would loop. Name the project repo that should receive the links |\n197: | A build is missing | Run the four `npm` commands in section 2 from the checkout, then rerun this guide. They also print as step 0 |\n198: | A step prints `n/a` as its class | That printed command has no route in the manifest yet. The command still works; only its class is unknown |\n199: | A line reads `local` or `foreign-link` | Left alone on purpose. Move that file or link yourself, or bring it under step 1's shape, and rerun the guide |\n200: | Only the steps are wanted | `list` prints them with no build check and no inspection |\n201: | A saved session lands in the checkout's `specs/` | The save ran from the Skilled checkout. Run it again from inside the target repo, as the caveat in section 7 says |\n202: | The project copies Skilled in instead of linking it | This guide covers linking only. A copy that keeps its own changes keeps them as overlays: run `/doctor:overlay status --root <copy>`, and see the overlays reference in `system-skilled-doctor` for adopting the engine when the project already has its own overlay folder |\n203: \n204: End interactive presentations with: `What would you like to do next?`"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-install-presentation.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1794851637360,"end":1794851637608}}}}
